\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Finite *-algebras, states and GNS}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{A state in the sense of the
\vocabk{real and complex algebraic states}{fdip-0024} is a normalized
positive functional. That note assumes no norm and no operator
representation. On a finite-dimensional *-algebra the functional
determines the GNS representation on the quotient by the left ideal
below. The value #{\omega(a^*a)} is the squared length of the class
of #{a}. The square root of that value is a seminorm on #{A} and a
norm on the quotient. A nonfaithful state, one whose left ideal is
nonzero, does not define a norm on all of #{A}. The dimension of the quotient is the rank of
the positive semidefinite form sending #{y} to the conjugate-linear
functional #{x\mapsto\omega(x^*y)}, and that rank equals the
codimension of the left ideal.}

\p{Let #{K=\mathbb R} or #{K=\mathbb C}, and let #{A} be a
finite-dimensional unital associative #{K}-algebra with an involution
of the kind fixed for a \vocabk{real algebraic state}{fdip-0024}
or a \vocabk{complex algebraic state}{fdip-0024}.
Let #{\omega} be a real
or complex algebraic state on #{A}, according to the scalar field:
#{K}-linear, normalized in the sense of a
\vocabk{normalized linear functional}{fdip-0021}, Hermitian positive, and satisfying
#{\omega(a^*a)\ge 0}. Define
#{\langle x,y\rangle=\omega(x^*y)}.
Let #{N} be the set of #{x} with #{\langle x,x\rangle=0}. The
quotient #{H_\omega=A/N} carries the descended inner product, the
operator #{\pi_\omega(a)} sends the class of #{b} to the class of
#{ab}, and #{\Omega} is the class of #{1}.}

\p{The form is positive semidefinite and Hermitian, and #{N} is a
left ideal. For the cyclic vector,
##{\langle\pi_\omega(a)\Omega,\pi_\omega(b)\Omega\rangle
=\omega(a^*b).}
After a basis #{(e_i)} of #{A} is declared, the Gram matrix
#{G_{ij}=\omega(e_i^*e_j)} satisfies
#{\dim H_\omega=\operatorname{rank} G}. The squared length of the
class of #{a} is #{\omega(a^*a)}.}

\p{Let #{A=\bigoplus_i\operatorname{End}_K(V_i)}, with each #{V_i} a
finite-dimensional #{K}-vector space, with componentwise composition
and with the adjoint acting on each block. After a basis of #{V_i}
is declared, #{\operatorname{End}_K(V_i)} is the matrix algebra
#{M_{m_i}(K)} with #{m_i=\dim V_i}, and the adjoint is conjugate
transpose. Suppose each #{\rho_i}
has the form #{\rho_i=c_i^*c_i}, and
#{\sum_i\trace(\rho_i)=1}. The functional
#{\omega(a)=\sum_i\trace(\rho_i a_i)} is a state, and
##{\dim H_\omega=\sum_i m_i\operatorname{rank}\rho_i.}}

\p{Example. On #{M_2}, the density #{\mathrm{diag}(2/3,1/3)} has rank #{2} and
gives dimension #{4}. The density #{\mathrm{diag}(1,0)} has rank
#{1} and gives dimension #{2}.}

\p{Let #{X} be a finite set and let #{A} be the algebra of functions
#{X\to K}, with pointwise product and pointwise conjugation. Let
#{w:X\to\mathbb R_{\geq 0}} satisfy #{\sum_x w(x)=1}, and set
#{\omega(f)=\sum_x w(x)f(x)}. Write #{\operatorname{supp} w} for the
labels with #{w(x)>0}. Then #{H_\omega} is the space of all functions
on that support, with the inner product weighted by #{w}. On a finite
set that space is #{\ell^2(\operatorname{supp} w)}.}

\p{[Equivariant precision and real types](fdip-004Y) records, inside a symmetry
sector, a matrix over the reals, the complexes or the quaternions.
This section does not prove that those matrices are the blocks of a
decomposition of the observable algebra.}
\proof{
\p{The form is linear in the second argument because #{\omega} is.}

\p{It is conjugate-linear in the first because
#{(\lambda x)^*=\overline\lambda\,x^*}.}

\p{It is Hermitian because #{\langle y,x\rangle} equals
#{\omega((x^*y)^*)}, and Hermitian positivity makes that the
conjugate of #{\omega(x^*y)}. The square
#{\langle x,x\rangle=\omega(x^*x)} is nonnegative by the state.}

\p{The inequality of Cauchy and Schwarz is
#{\lvert\langle x,y\rangle\rvert^2\le\langle x,x\rangle\langle y,y\rangle}.
Over #{\mathbb C}, if #{\langle x,y\rangle} is not zero, choose a
scalar #{u} of modulus #{1} with
#{u\langle x,y\rangle=\lvert\langle x,y\rangle\rvert}, and replace
#{y} by #{uy}. The new square #{\langle y,y\rangle} is unchanged, and
the new inner product is real and nonnegative. Over #{\mathbb R} no
phase is required. For every real #{t},
##{\langle x+ty,x+ty\rangle
=\langle x,x\rangle+2t\langle x,y\rangle+t^2\langle y,y\rangle\ge 0.}
If #{\langle y,y\rangle=0}, the values #{2t\langle x,y\rangle} cannot
stay above #{- \langle x,x\rangle} for every real #{t} unless
#{\langle x,y\rangle=0}. If #{\langle y,y\rangle>0}, complete the
square. The same quadratic is
##{\langle y,y\rangle\Bigl(t+\frac{\langle x,y\rangle}{\langle y,y\rangle}\Bigr)^2
+\langle x,x\rangle-\frac{\langle x,y\rangle^2}{\langle y,y\rangle}.}
The squared term can be made zero, so the remaining term is
nonnegative. Thus the inequality holds. Over #{\mathbb C}, putting
the unimodular factor back into #{y} multiplies #{\langle x,y\rangle}
by a scalar of modulus #{1} and does not change #{\langle y,y\rangle}.}

\p{If #{\langle x,x\rangle=0}, the inequality forces
#{\langle x,y\rangle=0} for every #{y}. Conversely the case #{y=x}
shows that the radical is exactly #{N}. It is a subspace. If
#{x,y\in N} and #{\lambda\in K}, then
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle x+\lambda y,x+\lambda y\rangle
={}&\langle x,x\rangle+\lambda\langle x,y\rangle\\
&+\overline{\lambda}\langle y,x\rangle
+\lvert\lambda\rvert^2\langle y,y\rangle.
\end{aligned}}
The two squares vanish because #{x,y\in N}. The two cross terms
vanish because an element of #{N} is orthogonal to every vector.
It is a left ideal. For #{b\in A} and #{x\in N},
#{\langle bx,bx\rangle=\omega(x^*b^*bx)=\langle x,(b^*b)x\rangle=0}.}

\p{The form therefore descends to the quotient. If #{x-x'} and
#{y-y'} lie in #{N}, then
#{\langle x,y\rangle-\langle x',y'\rangle
=\langle x-x',y\rangle+\langle x',y-y'\rangle}.
The first summand vanishes because #{x-x'} is in the radical. The
second is the conjugate of #{\langle y-y',x'\rangle}, which vanishes
for the same reason. The descended form is positive definite, and
finite dimensionality makes the quotient a Hilbert space with no
further completion. Its dimension is #{\dim A-\dim N}.}

\p{On a basis #{e_1,\ldots,e_n}, the matrix
#{G_{ij}=\langle e_i,e_j\rangle=\omega(e_i^*e_j)} is Hermitian. A
vector #{v} represents #{x=\sum_i v_i e_i}. Hermitian symmetry gives
#{G_{ji}=\overline{G_{ij}}}. The number
#{\langle x,e_j\rangle=\sum_i\overline{v_i}G_{ij}}
vanishes for every #{j} if and only if #{x\in N}: the forward
direction was proved for every vector, and the converse is linearity
in the second argument. Conjugating those
sums and replacing #{\overline{G_{ij}}} by #{G_{ji}} produces
#{(Gv)_j=0}. Rank-nullity
gives #{\operatorname{rank} G=n-\dim N=\dim H_\omega}. The class of
#{a} has squared length #{\omega(a^*a)}. The operator
#{\pi_\omega(a)} is well defined because #{N} is a left ideal, and
#{\langle\pi_\omega(a)\Omega,\pi_\omega(b)\Omega\rangle
=\langle a,b\rangle=\omega(a^*b)}.}

\p{On the direct sum, the product and the involution act blockwise, so
#{\omega(a^*a)=\sum_i\trace(\rho_i a_i^*a_i)}. The trace is
invariant under a cyclic permutation, and
#{\trace(b^*b)} is the sum of the squared moduli of the entries
of #{b}. Hence
##{\trace(\rho_i a_i^*a_i)
=\trace(c_i a_i^*a_i c_i^*)
=\trace\bigl((a_i c_i^*)^*(a_i c_i^*)\bigr),}
which is nonnegative. The functional is therefore positive. It is
normalized because #{\omega(1)=\sum_i\trace(\rho_i)=1}. Each
#{\rho_i=c_i^*c_i} is self-adjoint. The trace of a conjugate
transpose is the conjugate of the trace, and
#{(a_i\rho_i)^*=\rho_i a_i^*}, so
#{\trace(\rho_i a_i^*)=\overline{\trace(\rho_i a_i)}}.
Thus #{\omega} is Hermitian.}

\p{The same identity shows that #{\omega(a^*a)=0} if and only if
#{a_i c_i^*=0} for every #{i}. Different blocks occupy
complementary summands, so
#{\dim N=\sum_i\dim\{a_i:a_i c_i^*=0\}}. A matrix vanishes on a
subspace precisely when it factors through the quotient by that
subspace. The image of #{c_i^*} has dimension #{\operatorname{rank} c_i^*}.
The matrices vanishing on it are determined by their values on a
complement, so their space has dimension
#{m_i\bigl(m_i-\operatorname{rank} c_i^*\bigr)}. The quotient of that
block therefore has dimension #{m_i\operatorname{rank} c_i^*}. Summing
over the blocks gives
#{\dim H_\omega=\sum_i m_i\operatorname{rank} c_i^*}.}

\p{It remains to match that rank with #{\operatorname{rank}\rho_i}.
For the standard inner product,
#{\|c_i v\|^2=\langle c_i^*c_i v,v\rangle}.
If #{c_i^*c_i v=0}, the right-hand side vanishes, so #{c_i v=0}.
The converse is immediate, and rank-nullity gives
#{\operatorname{rank}\rho_i=\operatorname{rank} c_i}. A minor of a
transpose is the transpose of a minor, and the determinant is
unchanged by transposition. A minor of a conjugate transpose is the
conjugate of a minor of the transpose. The largest size of a nonzero
minor is therefore the same for #{c_i}, #{c_i^T} and #{c_i^*}. Hence
#{\operatorname{rank} c_i^*=\operatorname{rank}\rho_i}, and
#{\dim H_\omega=\sum_i m_i\operatorname{rank}\rho_i}. On #{M_2},
#{\mathrm{diag}(2/3,1/3)} is the square of
#{\mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{2/3},\sqrt{1/3})}, so its rank is #{2}.
#{\mathrm{diag}(1,0)} is the square of #{\mathrm{diag}(1,0)}, and
its only nonzero minor has size #{1}. The formula gives dimensions
#{4} and #{2}.}

\p{On functions, the indicator basis is orthogonal for the Gram form.
Indeed #{\omega(1_x^*1_y)=\delta_{xy}w(x)}, so the Gram matrix is
diagonal with entries #{w(x)}. Its rank is the number of strictly
positive weights. A function lies in #{N} exactly when it vanishes
on #{\operatorname{supp} w}, because
#{\omega(f^*f)=\sum_x w(x)\lvert f(x)\rvert^2}. Two functions
determine the same class exactly when they agree on that support, and
every function on the support arises in this way. The descended inner
product is
##{\langle f,g\rangle
=\sum_{w(x)>0}w(x)\overline{f(x)}\,g(x).}
Every function on a finite set is square-summable, so the space is
#{\ell^2(\operatorname{supp} w)}.}
}
